<sn><md>### The Seven Layers

![img](https://encrypted-tbn0.gstatic.com/images?q=tbn:ANd9GcS90HSxShmQjd3yCphemVKj2N1bX-k21CFCV_vZtKQy1w&amp;s=10)

+++ ...
![img](https://www.infosectrain.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/OSI-Model-Layers-1.jpg)
+++

| # | Layer | What it does | Data unit | Examples |
|---|-------|--------------|-----------|----------|
| 7 | **Application** | `Interface` for network services used by software | Data | HTTP, HTTPS, FTP, SMTP, DNS |
| 6 | **Presentation** | `Translates, encrypts, and compresses` data | Data | TLS/SSL, JPEG, ASCII, MPEG |
| 5 | **Session** | `Opens, manages, and closes` sessions between applications | Data | NetBIOS, RPC, session tokens |
| 4 | **Transport** | End-to-end delivery, segmentation, error recovery, flow control | Segment (TCP) / Datagram (UDP) | TCP, UDP |
| 3 | **Network** | Logical addressing and routing between networks | Packet | IP, ICMP, routers |
| 2 | **Data Link** | `Node-to-node` delivery on the same network, MAC addressing, error detection | Frame | Ethernet, Wi-Fi (802.11), switches |
| 1 | **Physical** | `Raw bit transmission` over a medium | Bits | Cables, fiber, hubs, radio signals |

A common mnemonic, top to bottom: **"All People Seem To Need Data Processing."** Bottom to top: **"Please Do Not Throw Sausage Pizza Away."**

</md></sn><sn><md>### OSI vs. TCP/IP

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-4
- TCP/IP has four layers: Application (OSI 5-7), Transport (4), Internet (3), and Network Access (1-2). 
- OSI is the `reference model` for understanding, while TCP/IP is what the internet actually uses.
:::

